LA  - en
AB  - With the rapid expansion of the global food supply chain and the increase in consumption patterns, efficient and reliable methods have been required to assess the nutritional quality and processing levels of food products. Conventional labeling techniques are heavily based on manual review and rule-based systems, which are time-consuming and prone to inconsistencies in nutrient data and ingredient representation. This paper presents a comprehensive machine learning-based framework that integrates two widely adopted health assessment methodologies. Nutri-Score prediction and NOVA classification. For Nutri-Score, we employ an XGBoost regressor trained on a dataset that includes more than 500,000 food products sourced from OpenFoodFacts. The model utilizes various features, such as the protein-to-fat ratio and the sugar-to-carbohydrate ratio, to predict a health score that ranges from-15 (healthiest) to +40 (unhealthiest). This model achieves an R² score of 0.9702 and a mean squared error (MSE) of 3.12. In parallel, we use a fine-tuned DistilBERT model trained on the GroceryDB data set of items greater than 10,500 to classify food items into NOVA processing levels (1 to 4) based on their ingredient lists, achieving a classification accuracy of 98.29%. This dual pipeline approach provides a scalable, automated, and cost-effective solution for food quality evaluation, supporting informed consumer decisions, nutrition policy development, and large-scale diet analysis.
