TY  - JOUR
TI  - Computational Semantic Mapping of Food Ontologies and Databases: An NLP and AI-Powered Framework for Precision Nutrition
AU  - Alejandro Márquez González
PY  - 2026
JO  - Archivo Digital UPM (Universidad Politécnica de Madrid)
UR  - https://openalex.org/W7168002541
LA  - en
AB  - This work is situated within the broader objective of automating food classification into the standardized FoodEx2 system, with the aim of improving data interoperability, food safety monitoring, and public health research. The related literature shows an evolution from supervised classification systems based on deep learning models to more flexible semantic retrieval approaches. More recent retrieval-augmented strategies decompose the task into sequential stages, such as base term identification, facet category prediction, and facet descriptor assignment. In this context, the present work focuses on a complementary problem: linking noisy, crowdsourced Open Food Facts ingredients to the FoodEx2 reporting hierarchy. The proposed framework combines semantic embeddings, lexical retrieval, Jaccard similarity, hierarchical beam search, and confidencebased scoring. The results show that, although professional systems achieve higher accuracy on controlled datasets, the proposed framework obtains reliable performance in a more challenging setting. Overall, these approaches demonstrate the value of hybrid semantic-lexical models and retrieval-based architectures for scalable and interpretable FoodEx2 coding.
