AB  - Color is a crucial sensory parameter that can distinguish between a food product’s appeal, acceptability, and interest with ease. In essence, it affects whether a food product is first accepted or rejected. The color of a product is determined by pigments. The avoidance of synthetic colorants by consumers has prompted the food industry to switch to natural pigments. Microbial pigments provide intriguing substitutes to synthetic pigments for various applications as they decompose more quickly and are more environmentally friendly. Fungi, microalgae, and bacteria are well known for producing a variety of naturally occurring pigments that differ noticeably in their chemical compositions, functions, stability, and solubility. Depending on their chemical arrangements, natural occurrence, and functional properties, they can be divided into different categories, such as derivatives of flavonoids, pyrroles, carotenoids, etc. The main pigments found in microbial sources that are used as food colorants are riboflavin, violacein, β-carotene, phycocyanin, canthaxanthin, prodigiosin, melanin, astaxanthin, and lycopene. The chemical structure of microbial pigments and the relationship between their structure and function require future investigations. This chapter describes the microbial food pigments applied in the food industry including dairy, bakery, and packaged foods.
