AB  - The diagnosis and management of metabolic diseases, particularly diabetes mellitus, rely heavily on the accurate measurement of glucose levels in body fluids. With the global prevalence of diabetes projected to increase substantially in the coming years, the demand for precise and real-time glucose monitoring is more critical than ever. This paper presents the development of a portable visible spectrophotometer tailored for glucose analysis, with a particular focus on its integration with digital microfluidics (DMF) platforms. The spectrophotometer utilizes colorimetric methods and microfluidic technology to enable rapid and efficient glucose detection. It features a userfriendly interface and a robust control and processing subsystem, making it suitable for point-of-care applications. Preliminary experimental validation demonstrates the device's optical performance using food dyes, while glucose assay measurements reveal the need for improved reaction protocols and voltage stabilization. This work highlights both the potential and current limitations of the proposed system for diabetes management, with specific engineering solutions identified to enhance reliability in clinical settings. Future improvements will focus on regulated light source drivers, heat insulation, and optimized glucose assay protocols to ensure consistent concentration-absorbance relationships.
