AB  - This study investigates a class-incremental learning strategy designed to mitigate catastrophic forgetting in Transformer-based architectures. The experiment evaluates a DistilBERT model trained on the Open Food Facts dataset to classify 100 distinct product categories introduced sequentially over 10 stages. The proposed methodology integrates Low-Rank Adaptation (LoRA) for parameter efficiency and an Experience Replay buffer to maintain long-term memory of previously seen classes without requiring task identity at inference. Hypotheses H1: A naïve sequential fine-tuning approach will suffer from severe catastrophic forgetting, resulting in a highly negative Backward Transfer (BWT) score and near-zero accuracy on early stages by the end of training. H2: The implementation of an Experience Replay buffer (retaining 50 samples per class) natively within the PyTorch data loading pipeline, combined with LoRA (rank=16), will significantly mitigate forgetting, yielding a higher Final Average Accuracy and a substantially improved BWT score compared to the baseline.
