AB  - Structuring Chinese recipes is inherently challenging due to their complex procedural logic and the semantic density of cooking instructions. This paper introduces the Chinese Recipe Flow Graph (CRFG), a novel dataset designed for fine-grained recipe modeling. Based on rigorous annotation protocols, we performed multi-round manual labeling of entities and relationxds within cooking texts. To bridge the gap between culinary actions and health metrics, Large Language Models (LLMs) were employed to map ingredients to the China Food Composition Table. Furthermore, we developed LogicFlow-Net, which utilizes Inter-sentence Position enhancement to capture long-range dependencies in complex procedural text. The resulting dataset comprises 304 recipes, 25,025 entities, 19,369 relation instances, and 727 ingredients with detailed nutritional data. Experimental results demonstrate that the proposed model achieves F1 scores of 82% and 91% for Named Entity Recognition (NER) and Relation Extraction (RE) tasks, respectively. Although the scale and diversity of the dataset require further expansion, this study achieves fine-grained modeling of recipe logic and nutritional data. Consequently, it provides high-quality data and methodological support for downstream applications such as intelligent cooking, nutrition management, and personalized recipe recommendation.
