AB  - Lack of publicly available DS composition databases can hinder estimates of total nutrient intakes & studies of DS & on health and disease risk This research is needed for estimating nutrient needs, informing policy decisions, and developing dietary guidance messages for consumers. Federal collaborators are currently working to produce publicly available DS databases. The Dietary Supplement Ingredient Database (DSID‐2) provides estimates for 20 nutrients based on chemical analyses from national samples of adult & child multivitamin‐minerals (MVM), and provides online calculators for researchers to compare estimated analytically derived data with product label values. OTC prenatal MVM & omega‐3 fatty acid products will be added to DSID in 2013. Another research gap is the lack of a label database; the NHANES database contains label derived nutrient information from DS consumed by survey respondents. And work is underway to create a label database of most DS sold in the US in collaboration with the National Library of Medicine. A controlled vocabulary (LanguaL) has been developed to link these databases. Advances in these databases to address research needs will be described. Funding: ODS/NIH;NLM/NIH; Nutrient Data Laboratory, USDA; and NCHS/CDC.
