AB  - The present study investigated the online availability and regulatory compliance of insect-based foods intended for human consumption within the European Union. Between May 2023 and October 2024, 454 authorized and 52 unauthorized insect products were purchased from international e-commerce platforms using a "mystery shopper" approach. Labels were evaluated against Regulation (EU) 1169/2011 and specific Novel Food Implementing Regulations. Results revealed critical non-compliance across both groups. Among authorized products, 99% exhibited labelling deficiencies, primarily involving lack of mandatory language translation (95%), improper allergen emphasis (11%), and non-indelible date marking (34%). Unauthorized species (e.g., scorpions, tarantulas, and giant water bugs) showed total non-compliance, lacking scientific names, net weight, nutritional declarations, and mandatory allergen warnings regarding cross-reactivity with crustaceans and dust mites. Statistical analysis confirmed significantly higher levels of non-compliance in unauthorized products (p < 0.001) regarding product identification, ingredient transparency, and traceability. Furthermore, 56% of unauthorized samples bore expiration dates preceding the purchase date, posing potential public health risks. Among the different countries and vendors of authorised products, differences in compliance regarding list of ingredients, instructions for use, language translation, lot number, scientific nomenclature, marketed 
