AB  - Proposed methodological framework for dietary polyphenol database development, adapted from Greenfield and Southgate (2003) and Charrondiere et al. (2012). This study's aim was to develop and validate a comprehensive South African dietary polyphenol database. This research was conducted in four sequential phases. In the first phase, polyphenol data were identified through a systematic review of South African literature, which included search strategy development, eligibility screening, data extraction and management, and data interpretation and reporting. For the second phase, a South African Dietary Polyphenol Data Quality Assessment Tool (SADPDQAT) was developed, validated, and applied to evaluate the quality of identified polyphenol literature and data. In the third phase, data from high-quality literature sources were extracted and compiled into a reference database following methods adapted from the USDA Flavonoid Database and Phenol-Explorer, guided by international standards provided by the International Network of Food Data Systems (INFOODS). The fourth phase involved validation of a representative sample of the compiled polyphenol data by chemical analysis. This involved food sampling and preparation, phenolic extraction, determination of gross and individual polyphenol contents, and statistical analysis of the literature data compared to the analytically generated polyphenol composition data from this study.
